{:edb/db-before-t 165 :edb/db-after-t 166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533478 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:44:24.707Z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399404 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087407 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399411 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse $3x^{2} + y^{2} - 6\\sqrt{3}x - 6y + 6 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088464 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x-6y+6=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088464 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}(0)-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)^{2}-9+6 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,3-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3+\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](images/de/de18c7e39dd73d5f72fd32b34b97b65cf0011e6da44b36a1d4b3d62df8852a9a.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186119765 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399624 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124493 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399417 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124532 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399603 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124557 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399610 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124604 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399617 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399370 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186147457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399377 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161995 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399384 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186161995 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399391 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399457 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399513 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399520 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399436 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399443 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399450 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162025 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399527 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162025 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399582 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161090 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399398 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x) =-x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}$, find its canonical (diagonal) form $y^{T}Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x = Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186162638 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186162638 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399506 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399534 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399589 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399596 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399541 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186166807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399569 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166010 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399576 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186167472 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186167472 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172034 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399492 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172034 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399548 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399555 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399562 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399464 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399471 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184205 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399478 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184205 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183877 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399424 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184883 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184883 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4-\\lambda  \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(20-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183883 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399430 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186184884 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184884 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399357 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1069 "q-94213" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+14{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1072 #uuid "190daf82-0a4f-5d1d-bcce-07f33dc7233c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+14{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+14{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}))+14{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2})+14{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}+14{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-9{x}_{3}^{2}+14{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-7{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group ${x}_{2}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{2},$ factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{2}^{2},$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-2({x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3})-7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-2([{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}]-4{x}_{3}^{2})-7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-2(({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2})-7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-2({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2}-7{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})^{2}-2({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399361 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1080 #uuid "10ef20bf-2e5d-50b3-b950-69a099e3e7b3" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1081 "${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1084 #uuid "50b9470b-2754-554a-a9c2-925ecf2d9815" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1081 "${x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1084 #uuid "79527219-621e-5317-b458-17d0d4848f04" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1081 "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1084 #uuid "5407e66c-